                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "230215177",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198421199537",
                            "num_reviews": 50,
                            "playtime_forever": 54,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 54,
                            "last_played": 1783778617
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I love this game on a conceptual level, and I want to like it, I really do. But something that the tags do not describe is that this game has what I like to call \"tightly timed puzzles\" -- you need to complete the puzzle within a tight timeframe, or otherwise you're back to the start. In this game, it is manifested by [spoiler]creatures that will shut down your lights[/spoiler], and while there is a pattern, there are so many of them in the rooms that it is of no much help. The abundance of them means that you are basically fighting against the clock regardless, much like in games such as Low Oxygen.\n\nIn the words of the great Gamechamp3000, \"I suck at video games\". And I hate when they make puzzles into timed things like this, even moreso because you may reach a point where they're unavoidable. I think I have reached one such point.\n\nI would also put the fact that you jump with the \"up\" button as a negative, because that makes your movements feel all the more cramped, but seeing how [spoiler]the entire point is that your jump improves when you turn on lights[/spoiler], it may be an intentional mechanic. I don't particularly agree with the philosophy of cramping the entire control scheme of your game to a tight corner of the keyboard to artificially inflate the difficulty, but YMMV.\n\nI also think it is worth noting that you cannot change the resolution from a small window. Not that I think that is a straight negative, but it will definitely be an unpleasant surprise for some.",
